```
ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: red
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS:
- Monitor's win vs difficulty (+0.095) is the trivially-expected gap between a time-resolved process signal (tool-error + looping + info-gain accumulating along the trajectory) and a single static task-feature predictor: information monotonically accrues with step index, so any non-degenerate online signal must beat a t=0 predictor. This is not evidence of a novel "structural progression signature" — it is the definition of online > offline information sets (Blackwell-style dominance).
- Monitor's "lead time" of +2.32 steps median is mechanically induced by the generative model: latent health drifts down BEFORE crossing the death threshold by construction, so any consistent readout of health-correlated observables MUST fire before the threshold crossing. Lead-time > 0 is a property of the simulator, not a discovered phenomenon.
- The flip at sigma_conf ~0.50 is an arithmetic identity: monitor AUC is constant (0.7427) because the monitor never reads confidence; the baseline AUC degrades monotonically with sigma_conf because more noise on a direct health readout → lower discrimination. The "flip-point" is just where two monotone curves cross — it carries no empirical content about real LLMs unless sigma_conf is calibrated to measured verbalized-confidence noise (it is not).
CONFOUNDS:
- sigma_conf is a free knob with no mapping to any measured LLM-confidence calibration statistic; sweeping it and finding "the monitor wins for large enough sigma_conf" is circular — you can engineer any flip-point by choosing the noise model. The cited verbalized-confidence AUROC band (0.5–0.7) is from DIFFERENT tasks/models than the simulator, so the "flip is within realistic range" mapping is a category transfer, not a calibration.
- Self-confidence in the sim is defined as a direct noisy readout of the SAME latent health that generates failure — so the confidence-vs-monitor comparison is rigged for confidence at low sigma_conf and for the monitor at high sigma_conf, by construction. Neither outcome tests the empirical claim.
- "Distinct from difficulty" conflates (i) the static-vs-dynamic information-set asymmetry (mundane) with (ii) the substantive claim that progression carries info orthogonal to difficulty axes a non-trivial difficulty predictor would extract. The L0's difficulty baseline is a single per-trajectory scalar — a strawman; a difficulty predictor that uses early-step features would close most of the +0.095 gap.
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED:
- Linear additive monitor (tool-error + looping − info-gain) is the right functional form; no ablation on which component carries the signal, no test of nonlinear/interaction terms.
- The latent-health → observables emission model (Gaussian readout, fixed noise) is unargued; real agent traces have heavy-tailed, bursty tool-error patterns that would change the monitor's calibration.
- Independence of sigma_conf from monitor noise (the sweep varies only baseline noise, not monitor noise) — a fair sweep would jointly perturb both and report the 2D frontier.
- The "lead time" metric assumes the agent's termination step is the ground-truth failure moment; in real traces failure is often diagnosable only post-hoc, making lead-time ill-defined.
STATISTICAL_GAPS:
- 8 seeds per cell is thin for CIs reported to 3 decimals; the CI [−0.011, −0.001] for the load-bearing confidence comparison spans an effect size indistinguishable from zero in any practical sense — calling this a "robust loss" overstates the resolution.
- No multiple-comparison correction across the 9-row sigma_conf sweep; the "flip-point" is a selected statistic.
- Lead-time coverage (42% < 0.5 pre-reg bar) is reported without a CI; with 96 rows × 400 trajectories the variance should be small but the miss vs pre-registration is the decisive negative and deserves explicit power analysis.
- No reproducibility check across alternative seeds for the prior-art-cited AUC ~0.9 (Mavi 2511.07364) — the comparison to "the prior art achieves 0.9" is taken at face value without checking whether that 0.9 is on comparable task distributions.
RATIONALE: The L0 follow-up is methodologically honest and the pre-registered ELSE branch fired correctly, but the underlying modeling exercise cannot, even in principle, settle the empirical claim: every positive result is a mechanical consequence of the simulator (online > static, lead-time > 0 by construction), and every negative result is a free-parameter choice (sigma_conf). The prior-art kill (Mavi 2511.07364 at AUC ~0.9 on online stepwise failure detection; Vasudev 2602.03338 at AUROC 0.94) is the load-bearing finding — and it does not depend on the sim at all. Under the claim's own framing (must beat self-confidence AND difficulty with measured lead time), the published online stepwise self-evaluators occupy exactly that niche at calibration levels (~0.9) where this L0's own model predicts the monitor LOSES. The 42% lead-time coverage already failed the pre-registered bar in EXP-0033. The "distinct from difficulty" residual win is the information-set-asymmetry tautology, not a substantive contribution. RED: relabeling of online stepwise failure detection with an unsupported lead-time framing; do not spend GPU L1.
```
